  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nToday...\n\nDrier weather and less humidity is expected today as a drier airmass \nmoves into the northern half of the CWA. Latest surface analysis has \na dry front draped through the southern portions of the CWA. North \nof the front surface dew points will drop into the 60s and PWATS \nwill drop below an inch and a half. The surface high will continue \nshifting east through Saturday resulting in a northeast to easterly \nwind. The large mid level ridge to our west will begin spreading \nback to the east over our CWA. Together these features will result \nin drier warmer conditions today./22/OAJ/\n\nTonight and Sunday...\n\nAn upper-lvl high over the Southwest & Texas will expand and \nprogress eastward towards the Southeast. A cut-off low pressure \ncenter near Gulf states will track westward, shifting rain chances \ninto the Pine Belt tomorrow. As the high progresses, heat indices \nare expected to break into the triple digits tomorrow; attributed \nmostly to higher humidity values.\n\nThe rest of the week...\n\nThe aforementioned high will settle over the Southeast CONUS around \nTuesday, lingering through the rest of the week. Throughout the week \ndiurnally driven showers and thunder are expected to progress slowly \nnorthward each day driven by increased moisture advection. As a \nresult of increased moisture/RH, dangerous heat concerns are \nexpected to return by mid-week -throughout areas that do not receive \nprecipitation- driving heat indices near or in excess of 105F. HWO \ngraphics may be needed but timing is still several days aways. \n\nAs always during this time of year, we will continue to monitor the \nGulf for any tropical system development. There are no tropical \nsystems forecast to impact forecast zones during the period./SW/OAJ/",
